/**

@page page_peano_space_filling_curves Space-filling curves

\tableofcontents

Peano orders its spacetree along a space-filling curve. The curve decides in
which order a refined cell's children are visited and which curve pattern each
child continues with. The grid uses the curve for this order only. The entity
data live in slot-addressed pools and the mesh assigns its slots in curve order.
Any curve that is a depth-first visit of every child once therefore yields a
correct mesh, and the curve is a compile-time choice of the library.

# Turtle grammars

Textbooks describe a space-filling curve as a grammar (Michael Bader,
Space-Filling Curves, Springer 2013). The non-terminals are the patterns in
which the curve can run through a cell. The terminals are turtle steps, the
arrows between two consecutive children. A production replaces one
non-terminal by a sequence of child non-terminals separated by turtle steps.
The two-dimensional Hilbert curve reads

    H -> A up H right H down B
    A -> H right A up A left C
    B -> C left B down B right H
    C -> B down C left C up A

and the Peano curve, with the axes ordered so that the curve runs along axis 0
first, reads

    P -> P right Q right P up R left S left R up P right Q right P
    Q -> Q right P right Q down S left R left S down Q right P right Q
    R -> R left S left R up P right Q right P up R left S left R
    S -> S left R left S down Q right P right Q down S left R left S

A grammar stores turtle steps only, never coordinates. The position of a child within its parent
follows from adding up the turtle steps that precede it.

# The grammar as C++

A struct derived from peano4::sfc::Grammar declares a curve. The base struct
contributes the turtle alphabet and the production() builder. The declaration
then reads like the textbook:

@code
struct peano4::sfc::HilbertGrammar final : peano4::sfc::Grammar {
  static constexpr int SubdivisionFactor{2};

  enum NonTerminal : std::uint8_t { H, A, B, C };

  static constexpr NonTerminal StartSymbol{H};

  static constexpr std::array Productions{
    production(H, A, Up, H, Right, H, Down, B),
    production(A, H, Right, A, Up, A, Left, C),
    production(B, C, Left, B, Down, B, Right, H),
    production(C, B, Down, C, Left, C, Up, A)
  };
};
@endcode

production() checks at compile time that children and steps alternate. Every
grammar declares its productions for two and for three dimensions. The curves
shipped with Peano are peano4::sfc::PeanoGrammar (the default, subdivision
factor 3) and peano4::sfc::HilbertGrammar (subdivision factor 2, with Bader's
twelve-symbol grammar in three dimensions).

# Compiling the grammar into tables

peano4::sfc::SpaceFillingCurve takes a grammar and derives the child-order
table from it while the compiler instantiates the class. For every
non-terminal, the turtle walk of its production yields the child positions and
the production's right-hand side yields the child states. The derivation runs
in constexpr functions. The table is a constant of the program. The mesh build
reads it with one indexed load per refined cell.

Three static assertions reject a faulty grammar before any code runs:

- every production sits at the index of its left-hand side,
- every production visits each of the SubdivisionFactor^d child cells once
  with unit steps inside the cell,
- the exit point of every child meets the entry point of its successor.

The third check derives the entry and exit points of every non-terminal as
the fixed point of the grammar's recursion: the entry point of a pattern is
the entry point of its first child, scaled into that child's position. A
mistyped child symbol in a production breaks this connection and fails the
build.

# The grid side

peano4::grid::AutomatonState stores the curve state of a cell next to its
level and geometry. peano4::grid::AutomatonState::refineState() reads the
children of that state from the active curve and derives every child's
geometry and curve state from its table entry. The cell connectivity build in
peano4::Spacetree and the rank partition in peano4::topology::Tree loop over
the same child sequence. The alias peano4::sfc::ActiveCurve selects the active
curve. CMake configures it from PEANO_WITH_SPACE_FILLING_CURVE:

    cmake .. -DPEANO_WITH_SPACE_FILLING_CURVE=Hilbert

The subdivision factor of the active curve decides how many children a
refined cell has along each axis. The grid, the block-structured interpolation
and restriction, the particle lifts and drops and the generated solver code
read peano4::sfc::ActiveCurve::SubdivisionFactor and
peano4::sfc::ActiveCurve::ChildrenPerCell. A Hilbert build bisects every cell.
A Peano build splits it in three. The Discontinuous Galerkin solvers derive
their interpolation and restriction matrices between a cell and its children
in C++ (exahype2/dg/Basis.h) from the quadrature nodes and the subdivision
factor. Every table of the Python code generator then serves every curve. The
3^d neighbourhood of a cell follows from the lattice alone.
peano4::CellsPerNeighbourhood names it.

# Adding a curve

Add a header under src/peano4/sfc/curves that defines the grammar and gives its
non-terminals the names of the textbooks. peano4::sfc::ActiveCurve builds
both the header name and the struct name from the curve name and the suffix
Grammar. Add the curve name to the PEANO_WITH_SPACE_FILLING_CURVE option in
cmake/PeanoOptions.cmake and instantiate peano4::sfc::SpaceFillingCurve with
the grammar in src/unittests/peano4/sfc/SpaceFillingCurveTest.cpp. The static
assertions of peano4::sfc::SpaceFillingCurve report a grammar that does not
describe a continuous space-filling curve at compile time.

*/
